---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="About - Saad Shahd">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-20">
    <h1 class="text-5xl font-bold mb-12">About</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
        I build systems for a reason—I chase the 'why' without fixating on the 'how.' Over 15 years, I developed a philosophy that makes the 'how' effortless.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        I'm a Senior Software Engineer at Wise, doing principal-level work on product discovery. Currently leading the editorial-platform project: a comprehensive content system enabling 20 teams to collaborate on wise.com without friction, inconsistency, or incorrectness. The challenge isn't just technical—it's solving global→local fintech at scale: regulations differ by market, offerings vary by country, and cultural nuances matter.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        My approach is rooted in functional programming principles I learned the hard way—through production bugs in high-velocity startup environments at Instabug, StatsBomb, and Wise. Traditional thinking says you trade velocity for technical debt. I learned that functional thinking eliminates that trade-off entirely. When you separate behavior from state and make illegal states unrepresentable, you move fast <em>and</em> stay confident.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        At StatsBomb, I designed a real-time collection system that reduced 90-minute match collection from 16 man-hours to 4 concurrent collectors. When we scaled to American football, the architecture didn't just survive—it thrived. I built a dataspec and DSL that let PMs define sport collection rules without engineering involvement. We never had to choose between velocity and correctness.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        I don't predict the future—the future is ambiguous. The best we can do is embrace that ambiguity and design lean systems that adapt without breaking.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
        These days, I'm exploring how LLMs introduce new probabilistic capabilities to our developer toolbelts. I work primarily in TypeScript, Python, Go, and Clojure.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg font-medium text-gray-800">
        What makes me different: Most engineers optimize the "how." I optimize the "why"—which makes the "how" a piece of cake.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-12 mb-6">Technical Expertise</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
        <li>Functional programming and separation of concerns</li>
        <li>Distributed systems correctness</li>
        <li>Real-time event processing and collection systems</li>
        <li>Content architecture and editorial platforms</li>
        <li>Global→local scaling (fintech regulations, i18n/l10n)</li>
        <li>Developer experience and platform thinking</li>
        <li>Team leadership and principal-level architectural decisions</li>
        <li>LLM-augmented development patterns</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-12 mb-6">Career Highlights</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-700">
        <li><strong>Editorial Platform at Wise:</strong> Led comprehensive content platform enabling 20 teams to collaborate on wise.com, solving global→local fintech content challenges at scale while maintaining design consistency and developer experience.</li>
        <li><strong>Real-Time Collection System at StatsBomb:</strong> Designed concurrent collection solution reducing 90-minute match collection from 16 man-hours to 4 concurrent collectors. Built dataspec + DSL empowering PMs to define sport collection rules without engineering involvement.</li>
        <li><strong>Startup to Enterprise Journey:</strong> High-velocity technical leadership across Instabug, StatsBomb, and Wise—learning functional architecture principles through production reality.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
